Temperature distribution in a cylindrical shell
===============================================

FILES
-----
cyl_shell.py   :: Python module to evaluate the analytical solution
cyl-T-dist.png :: example output figure

PREREQUISITES
-------------


I built a Python virtual environment to run the script, and installed the following packages:

> pip install sympy numpy matplotlib

This grabbed other dependencies, resulting in a virtual environment with:

Package         Version
--------------- -----------
contourpy       1.3.0
cycler          0.12.1
fonttools       4.54.1
kiwisolver      1.4.7
matplotlib      3.9.2
mpmath          1.3.0
numpy           2.1.2
packaging       24.1
pillow          11.0.0
pyparsing       3.2.0
python-dateutil 2.9.0.post0
six             1.16.0
sympy           1.13.3

The code should work with any relatively recent versions of packages.
These precise package versions are not required.


NOTES
-----

1. The analytical solution is taken from Example 4.4 in Hahn and Ozisik (2012).
   Their cylindrical shell sweeps in angle (radians) from phi = 0 to some end point phi = phi0,
   and has an inner radius (a) and an outer radius (b).
   Boundary conditions (BCs) are:
   imposed flux (as function of phi) on outer shell
   insulation (zero flux) on cut boundaries of shell
   T = 0 on inner boundary (T = 0 is a convenient choice for analytical solution in terms of BC treatment)

2. The analytical solution is actually a sum to infinity.
   The sum converges quite quickly.
   The default number of terms for the sum is set to 10.
   Accuracy is tied to number of terms so it might be worth a small experiment with number of terms
   to get a desired accuracy.
   The number of terms is controlled as a class variable _n_terms (but could be made caller configurable if desired)

3. Python class gives solution for temperature as a function of radius (r) and angle (theta) -- in radians.
   The example assumes pi/2 <= theta <= pi.
   The user sets an inner temperature for the cylinder (T_i)
   The user supplys the flux as a function PHI (so they'll need to map theta back to PHI)

4. The example inputs are designed to mimic the physical situation in an experiment by Wieting (1987),
   and this is an Eilmer test case described at:
   https://gdtk.uqcloud.net/docs/eilmer/eilmer-catalog-for-hugo/#_2dcht_hollow_cylindertransient_fluid_transient_solid

